  "_cap_comment": "TWO CAPS, TWO JOBS. 'perBrand' is the WALK DEPTH: how far into each store build_catalog.py looks and records, i.e. the measurement. 'perBrandDisplay' is how much of that ships in catalog.json, i.e. what the app downloads and what becomes a cutout and an embedding. They were ONE number (60) until 2026-08-06, and because /products.json returns published_at DESCENDING that made the tracked shelf a rotating 60-piece PUBLISHING FRONT rather than a catalogue: measured live on 2026-08-06 by walking the whole roster to exhaustion (157 of 161 stores answered), the median store publishes 157 eligible pieces (p75 324, p90 638, p95 897, max 3,104; 45,718 in total) and only 19% of stores fit inside 60 — 81% are bigger. For the rest, a new listing pushed an old piece out of the window and the old piece read as having left the market: 981 of 2,041 disappearances between 2026-07-16 and 2026-08-01, 48%, were whole-brand rotation. 750 = three Shopify pages of 250, the smallest whole number of pages that clears the roster's 90th percentile (638); it fully covers 93% of stores (146 of 157), against 19% at a cap of 60. The display cap stays 60 because catalog.json is 1.09 KB/product and is served to phones by jsDelivr (~20 MB/file ceiling), and every product in it also becomes a ~111 KB cutout blob on a repo already at 948 MB of GitHub's 5 GB. Raising THIS number is what would take the app off the air; raising the walk depth is free. The full walk is published in shelf.json.",
  "perBrand": 750,
  "perBrandDisplay": 60,
  "_retailers_comment": "PARTNER RETAILERS — real shops we've partnered with that stock many designers. Items keep the DESIGNER as the brand (the taste engine learns designers, not shops) and carry a 'retailer' stamp the app resolves against catalog.retailers for the tile tint, badge and in-app store block. 'productTypes' scopes what we ingest (Loupe is a women's app — a boutique also sells menswear, candles and books). 'perVendorCap' stops one high-volume label eating the whole allocation. Set 'enabled': false to switch a partnership off in one edit — items disappear on the next refresh, no app release needed.",
